messages:
maya: Quick check — running into a weird mismatch between tool schema and typed SDKs when funcalls return additional fields. Example: tool declares { action: string, result: { text: string } } but runtime returns { action, result, meta: { latency: 12 } } and TS/Go clients blow up on strict decoding. Anyone dealt with this pattern?

sam: Yeah, seen that. Two common approaches: (1) loosen SDK decoders to accept extra fields (allow unknowns), (2) keep strict server-side validation and write a small shim that strips unknowns before client decode.

maya: We're trying to avoid server shims in hosted mode — prefer client helpers. For TS we can use zod .passthrough(), but what about Go and Ruby?

ali: Go: use mapstructure/json.RawMessage for unknowns, or generate structs with `json:"-"` fallback — but that's awkward. I wrote a tiny helper that post-processes the raw json and removes keys not in the expected schema before unmarshalling. Works but ugly.

jonah: For Ruby we just use Hashie::Mash and ignore unknown keys in most places. But for funcall typed responses we want schema safety. Could do validation step on the client that runs the model/tool response through the model schema and returns a typed object + warnings for unknowns.

maya: Thinking about an SDK-level "coerceToSchema" utility: takes the runtime JSON and a schema descriptor (lightweight list of allowed keys or a JSON Schema) and returns sanitized JSON that safe-decodes with the language's generated types. Would that be acceptable?

sam: Sounds reasonable. Two questions: (a) how do we represent schema descriptors consistently across languages, (b) do we apply it automatically for funcalls only or expose opt-in hooks?

ali: For (a) I'd reuse the existing function schema we ship in the OpenAPI-ish manifest. It's already available in the model routing metadata. Keep descriptor as a small projection (allowed keys + types). For (b) default to opt-in with telemetry flag, but provide a policy: "strip_unknowns": true/false, plus a failure mode (warn | error | coerce).

maya: Draft of the TS helper I had in mind — super tiny:
```ts
// coerce.ts
export function coerceToSchema(obj: any, schema: Record<string, any>): any {
  if (!obj || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
  const out: any = Array.isArray(obj) ? [] : {};
  for (const k of Object.keys(schema)) {
    if (k in obj) {
      const s = schema[k];
      if (s.type === 'object') out[k] = coerceToSchema(obj[k], s.properties || {});
      else out[k] = obj[k]; // simple types: assume fine
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

jonah: That's clean. If we add this to SDKs we should also emit a structured log when keys are dropped (so customers can audit missing fields). devx-bot: we have a telemetry topic for schema-coercion already.

devx-bot: Note: added `telemetry.schema_coercion_drops` counter to the devx metrics backlog (ticket #DF-1729).

sam: For Go I'd propose: accept raw json as map[string]any, run a small pruning function using a schema described as map[string]SchemaDesc, then json.Marshal + json.Unmarshal into the typed struct. Slight perf hit but rare for funcall outputs. We can optimize later with streaming decoders.

maya: Perf is a concern because some customers stream tools back large artifacts. We should only coerce for structured tool responses (short JSON blobs), not arbitrary streams. Add a size threshold and only coerce if < N KB.

ali: +1. Also add an opt-in header for private deployments so teams can disable telemetry.

maya: OK — plan: 1) spec for schema descriptor (small JSON schema subset), 2) TS/Go/Ruby helpers implementing coerceToSchema + logging, 3) SDK opt-in config + size threshold, 4) add tests for unknown field preservation vs drop. I'll open a PR with the TS helper and tests.

sam: Ping here when PR is up — we can copy pattern to Go/Ruby. Also add a small e2e: tool returns extra meta and we assert downstream typed client sees no exception and receives only declared fields.

maya: PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/432 (draft). PTAL for approach and naming.

ali: Reviewing now. Quick suggestion: name the helper `sanitizeForType` and add an option param `{ mode: 'coerce'|'warn'|'error', maxBytes?: number }`.

jonah: Also document behavior with streaming toolcall code paths — if streaming is enabled we skip coercion and instead emit a final validation pass when the stream ends. Add a note in docs.

maya: Good call. I'll update PR. Thanks all — will follow up with Go seed PR after TS lands. :thumbsup:

